# courses-business.kb - Courses / Business
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 4 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

business course | definition | A business course teaches how organisations are run, and the subject has shifted noticeably from accounting and structure towards customers and behaviour over the last few decades. What it does not teach is a business; the practical part comes from actually operating one, and most graduates first learn that by running something small or working in sales. The transferable part is a way of thinking about customers, cost and cash rather than a technical skill, which is why it is popular with people who change field. | business course, business, organisation, customer, cash, graduate | what is a business course, is business worth studying, is business hard | business course | business
management | definition | Management is the work of coordinating other people towards a result, and the definition hides the difficulty, which is that you do not control the output. The functions are planning, organising, leading and controlling, and the last two are the ones people are hired for. Managers spend most of their time in communication and decision-making rather than doing the technical work, and the transition is genuinely hard because being good at the work is what got them promoted. | management, manage, coordinate, plan, lead, control | what is management, what does a manager do, is management a good career | management | pamamahala
marketing | definition | Marketing is finding out what people want and then communicating it in a way they act on, and the discipline as taught covers research, positioning, pricing and promotion. The part students find dull is the part that matters, because a campaign that runs before anyone has checked whether the product has a market will work and fail for reasons that have nothing to do with creativity. Modern practice puts more weight on measuring what happened after than on what was intended before. | marketing, market, customer, research, price, promotion | what is marketing, is marketing still relevant, what does a marketer do | marketing | marketing
startup | definition | A startup is a company built to search for a repeatable business model rather than to execute a known one, which is a definition with real consequences rather than a synonym for a small firm. It means the business is expected to change, the finances are planned around raising money rather than profit, and the team is small. The phrase unicorn refers to a private company valued above a billion dollars, and the number of them is small enough that the label is close to meaningless in aggregate. | startup, company, model, repeatable, valuation, raise | what is a startup, what is a unicorn, is a startup just a small business | startup | startup
